derstand the special value of this infinitesimal fraction dotted among the amorphous masses.\nL87: In the same way, the meaning of a mathematical notion is by no means confined to its formal definition; in fact, it may be rather better expressed by a (generally fairly small) sample of the basic examples, which serve the mathematician as the motivation and the substantive definition, and at the same time as the real meaning of the notion.\nL88: ### Similar content being viewed by others\nL89: \nL90: cite16†Image†media.springernature.com L91: \nL92: ### cite17†Encountering Algebraic Reasoning in Contemporary Classrooms: Epilogue L93: \nL94: Chapter © 2019\nL95: \nL96: cite18†Image†media.springernature.com L97: \nL98: ### cite19†Making Implicit Algebraic Thinking Explicit: Exploiting National Characteristics of German Approaches L99: \nL100: Chapter © 2018\nL101: \nL102: cite20†Image†media.springernature.com L103: ### cite21†e-Collaborative Forums as Mediators When Solving Algebraic Problems L104: \nL105: Chapter © 2017\nL106: \nL107: Search within this book\nL108: \nL109: [Input] Search [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL110: ## Table of contents (22 chapters)\nL111: \nL112:   1. ### Front Matter\nL113: \nL114: Pages I-5\nL115: \nL116: cite22†Download chapter PDF L117: \nL118:   2. ### cite23†What is Algebra? L119: \nL120:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL121: \nL122: Pages 6-11\nL123: \nL124:   3. ### cite24†Fields L125: \nL126:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL127: \nL128: Pages 11-17\nL129: \nL130:   4. ### cite25†Commutative Rings L131: \nL132:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL133: \nL134: Pages 17-23\nL135: \nL136:   5. ### cite26†Homomorphisms and Ideals L137: \nL138:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL139: \nL140: Pages 24-33\nL141: \nL142:   6. ### cite27†Modules L143: \nL144:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL145: \nL146: Pages 33-40\nL147: \nL148:   7. ### cite28†Algebraic Aspects of Dimension L149:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL150: \nL151: Pages 41-50\nL152: \nL153:   8. ### cite29†The Algebraic View of Infinitesimal Notions L154: \nL155:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL156: \nL157: Pages 50-61\nL158: \nL159:   9. ### cite30†Noncommutative Rings L160: \nL161:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL162: \nL163: Pages 61-74\nL164: \nL165:   10. ### cite31†Modules over Noncommutative Rings L166: \nL167:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL168: \nL169: Pages 74-79\nL170: \nL171:   11. ### cite32†Semisimple Modules and Rings L172: \nL173:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL174: \nL175: Pages 79-90\nL176: \nL177:   12. ### cite33†Division Algebras of Finite Rank L178: \nL179:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL180: \nL181: Pages 90-96\nL182:   13. ### cite34†The Notion of a Group L183: \nL184:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL185: \nL186: Pages 96-108\nL187: \nL188:   14. ### cite35†Examples of Groups: Finite Groups L189: \nL190:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL191: \nL192: Pages 108-124\nL193: \nL194:   15. ### cite36†Examples of Groups: Infinite Discrete Groups L195: \nL196:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL197: \nL198: Pages 124-140\nL199: \nL200:   16. ### cite37†Examples of Groups: Lie Groups and Algebraic Groups L201: \nL202:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL203: \nL204: Pages 140-151\nL205: \nL206:   17. ### cite38†General Results of Group Theory L207: \nL208:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL209: \nL210: Pages 151-160\nL211:   18. ### cite39†Group Representations L212: \nL213:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL214: \nL215: Pages 160-177\nL216: \nL217:   19. ### cite40†Some Applications of Groups L218: \nL219:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL220: \nL221: Pages 177-188\nL222: \nL223:   20. ### cite41†Lie Algebras and Nonassociative Algebra L224: \nL225:      * Igor R. Shafarevich\nL226: \nL227: Pages 188-201\nL228: \nL229: cite42†Back to top L230: ## Reviews\nL231: \nL232: From the reviews:\nL233: \nL234: \"This is one of the few mathematical books, the reviewer has read from cover to cover ... The main merit is that nearly on every page you will find some unexpected insights...\" (Zentralblatt für Mathematik und Ihre Grenzgebiete, 1991)\nL235: \"...which I read like a novel and undoubtedly will become a classic. ... A merit of the book under review is that it contains several important articles from journals which are not all so easily accessible. ... Furthermore, at the end of the book, there are some Notes by the author which are indispensible for the necessary historical background information. ... This valuable book should be on the shelf of every algebraist and algebraic geometer.\" (Nieuw Archief voor Wiskunde, 1992)\nL236: \"... There are few proofs in full, but there is an exhilarating combination of sureness of foot and lightness of touch in the exposition ... which transports the reader effortlessly across the whole spectrum of algebra.... The challenge to Ezekiel, \"Can these bones live?\" is, all too often, the reaction of students when introduced to the bare bones of the concepts and constructs of modern algebra.\nL237: Shafarevich's book - which reads as comfortably as an extended essay - breathes life into the skeleton and will be of interest to many classes of readers...\" (The Mathematical Gazette, 1991)\nL238: \"... According to the preface, the book is addressed to \"students of mathematics in the first years of an undergraduate course, or theoretical physicists or mathematicians from outside algebra wanting to get an impression of the spirit of algebra and its place in mathematics.\" I think that this promise is fully justified.\nL239: The beginner, the experts and also the interested scientist who had contact with algebraic notions - all will read this exceptional book with great pleasure and benefit.\" (Zeitschrift für Kristallographie, 1991)\nL240: \"This is a truly wonderful book, one that anyone teaching algebra should read and which should be pointed out to talented students, particularly those who want to know a little more about what and why abstract algebra is. This book is volume 1 in the Algebra section of the Springer Encyclopedia of Mathematical Sciences … . The examples are particularly well chosen, simple enough to understand… .\nL241: one that will enrich your understanding of algebra and deepen your knowledge of mathematics as a whole.\" (Fernando Q. Gouvêa, MathDL, March, 2007)\nL242: ## Authors and affiliations\nL243: \nL244:   * ### Steklov Mathematical Institute, Russian Academy of Science, Moscow, Russia\nL245: \nL246: Igor R. Shafarevich\nL247: \nL248: ## Accessibility information\nL249: ### PDF accessibility summary\nL250: This PDF is Not accessible. It is based on scanned pages and does Not support features such as screen reader compatibility or descriptions for Non-text content (e.g., images and graphs). However, it likely supports searchable and selectable text based on OCR (Optical Character Recognition). Users with accessibility needs may Not be able to use this content effectively. If you have a question or an access need, please get in touch with us via https://www.springernature.com/gp/info/accessibility\nL251: ## Bibliographic information\nL252: \nL253:   * Book title: Basic Notions of Algebra\nL254: \nL255:   * Authors: Igor R. Shafarevich\nL256: \nL257:   * Series title: cite10†Encyclopaedia of Mathematical Sciences L258: \nL259:   * DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/b137643\nL260: \nL261:   * Publisher: Springer Berlin, Heidelberg\nL262: \nL263:   * eBook packages: cite43†Mathematics and Statistics , cite44†Mathematics and Statistics (R0) L264: \nL265:   * Copyright information: Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2005\nL266: \nL267:   * Hardcover ISBN: 978-3-540-25177-4 Published: 13 April 2005\nL268:   * Softcover ISBN: 978-3-642-42516-5 Published: 20 November 2014\nL269: \nL270:   * eBook ISBN: 978-3-540-26474-3 Published: 15 August 2005\nL271: \nL272:   * Series ISSN: 0938-0396\nL273: \nL274:   * Edition number: 1\nL275: \nL276:   * Number of pages: IV, 260\nL277: \nL278:   * Add